        <OtherAbstract Type="PIP" Language="eng">
            <AbstractText>A review of the epidemiological literature appears to show a small, but significant, increased risk of vascular complications with oral contraceptive (OC) use. Possible limitations of these studies are mentioned. In fact, even before widespread OC use, an association between estrogen ingestion and thromboembolism had been made. Studies showing a relationship between OC use and incidence of the following conditions are reviewed: 1) venous thromboembolism including pulmonary embolus; 2) coronary thrombosis; 3) cerebrovascular disease; and 4) other vascular disorders. There is growing evidence that the correlation between OC use and vascular risk has to do with OC estrogen content. Smoking enhances the risk, as does age of the user. Few studies have been done into the relationship between risk and duration of OC use. Current, rather than past, use seems to be the risk factor. Numerous etiologies have been proposed whereby OCs place the patient at greater thrombotic risk. It must be remembered, however, that, although the relative risk may be increased, the actual risk is still quite small. A lowered hormone content reduces the risk. OCs remain a safe and effective contraceptive if contraindications are remembered.</AbstractText>
        </OtherAbstract>
